PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains unanimous consensus on 23 states as the minimum size for a strong coalition. Under worst-case adversarial timing beginning late in 2000, staggered six-year Senate terms prevent the coalition from achieving the requisite quorums or supermajorities for any Article IV congressional statutory pathways within 51 months. Consequently, the coalition must utilize the Article V state-legislative convention route, which completely turns over across two biennial election cycles (48 months) and convenes within the operational window. Game-theoretically modeling interstate population migration under Census 2000 demographics—incorporating the populations of Puerto Rico and the Federal District into the adversary's mobile pool—yields a threshold requiring over 50/63 of the national population, which is satisfied exclusively by the top 23 most populous states.
}

